# EKG Heartbeat Classification with a 1D CNN (PyTorch)

Classifies individual heartbeats from EKG signals into 5 standard AAMI
categories (Normal, Supraventricular, Ventricular, Fusion, Unknown/Paced)
using the MIT-BIH Arrhythmia Database and a 1D convolutional neural network.

## Files
- `dataset.py` — loads/segments heartbeats from the real MIT-BIH database
  (via the `wfdb` package) or generates synthetic ECG-like data for testing
  the pipeline without internet access.
- `model.py` — `ECGNet`, a small 1D CNN (~11K parameters) built for
  classifying short heartbeat windows.
- `train.py` — trains the model, evaluates it, and prints accuracy,
  a confusion matrix, and per-class precision/recall/F1.

## How to run

### Option A — Real data (recommended, needs internet access to physionet.org)
```bash
pip install torch wfdb numpy
python train.py --source real --epochs 15
```
On first run, `wfdb` will download the 48-record MIT-BIH Arrhythmia
Database directly from PhysioNet (a few minutes). This gives you real,
citable results on a well-known benchmark dataset.

### Option B — Synthetic data (no internet needed, sandbox/demo mode)
```bash
python train.py --source synthetic --epochs 12
```
Generates ~4,000 synthetic heartbeat waveforms with the same 5-class
structure to verify the full pipeline (data → model → training →
evaluation) runs correctly. **This is not real patient data** — use it
only to sanity-check the code, not to report results.

## What the model does
- Input: a 180-sample window centered on each annotated heartbeat.
- Architecture: 3 stacked 1D conv blocks (16→32→64 channels) with batch
  norm, ReLU, and max-pooling, followed by global average pooling and a
  small fully-connected classifier head.
- Output: probability distribution over 5 heartbeat classes.

## Results (synthetic pipeline-validation run, 12 epochs)
This run confirms the code trains and evaluates correctly:
- Test accuracy: **99.3%**
- Near-perfect precision/recall across all 5 classes

These numbers are inflated because the synthetic classes are cleanly
separable by design — they are **not** a claim about real-world EKG
classification performance.

## Expected results on real MIT-BIH data
Published work using similar 1D CNN architectures on MIT-BIH typically
reports:
- ~97-99% accuracy on binary (normal vs. abnormal) classification
- ~90-97% accuracy on the full 5-class AAMI classification, with lower
  recall on minority classes (Fusion, Unknown/Paced) due to class imbalance

To get real numbers: run `python train.py --source real` from an
environment with internet access (e.g., your own machine, Google Colab,
or a Jupyter environment), and report the test set accuracy, confusion
matrix, and per-class F1 that get printed at the end.

## Extending this project
- Add data augmentation (jitter, time-warping) to help minority classes.
- Try a class-weighted loss function to address the imbalance seen in
  MIT-BIH (Normal beats vastly outnumber Fusion/Unknown beats).
- Compare against the transformer-based and wavelet-based approaches
  referenced in prior literature review work on this topic.